; ARBITRARY PRECISION ON GO, WHICH IS `math/big`.
;
; ADR 0019's third escape: an integer operation the compiler cannot prove stays
; inside the portable window is a compile error, and one of the three ways to
; clear it is to declare a range ABOVE the window, which promotes that value to
; arbitrary precision. This file is what that promotion lands on here.
;
; It is a TARGET DECLARATION and not a runtime we ship, which is the whole
; parasite model: three of the four hosts already have a bignum, so the bar
; general-purpose.md set — "beyond the window they cost what they would cost if
; we implemented them on the target" — is met by construction on those three.
; windows ships none and declares none, and covering says so.
;
; THE SEMANTICS MUST BE THE LANGUAGE'S, not Go's convenience. `Quo`/`Rem`
; truncate toward zero and take the dividend's sign, which is what
; integers.md §4 measured all four hosts agreeing on inside the window;
; `Div`/`Mod` are Euclidean and would make the SAME source disagree with itself
; either side of 2^53. That is the one place a bignum can silently change an
; answer, and it is chosen here rather than inherited.
;
; A KNOWN COST, recorded rather than discovered later: `new(big.Int).Add(a, b)`
; allocates a fresh receiver per operation, which is exactly the naive form
; bigarith-2026-08-28 measured at 4-5x worse than a careful one, and 3.97x worse
; than a fixed-limb `build`. That is the rung ABOVE this one -- a finite range
; gives a limb count, which gives a `build` of known length, which gives zero
; allocations -- and it is not built here. What is built here is correctness at
; the host's own price, which is the bar this target has to clear first.

(target go
  ; Not `(type big …)` by accident: `big` is the type language's name for the
  ; top of the representation ladder, and `*big.Int` is Go's spelling of it. The
  ; pointer is part of the spelling because `big.Int` is a struct whose zero
  ; value is usable but whose methods take a receiver.
  (type big "*big.Int")

  (module go
    (prim big+ (big big) big expr "new(big.Int).Add(%s, %s)" pure (import "math/big"))
    (prim big- (big big) big expr "new(big.Int).Sub(%s, %s)" pure (import "math/big"))
    (prim big* (big big) big expr "new(big.Int).Mul(%s, %s)" pure (import "math/big"))
    ; Quo and Rem, NOT Div and Mod -- see the header.
    (prim big/ ((a big) (b big)) big expr "new(big.Int).Quo(%s, %s)" pure (import "math/big"))
    (prim big% ((a big) (b big)) big expr "new(big.Int).Rem(%s, %s)" pure (import "math/big"))

    (prim big<  (big big) bool expr "%s.Cmp(%s) < 0"  pure)
    (prim big<= (big big) bool expr "%s.Cmp(%s) <= 0" pure)
    (prim big>  (big big) bool expr "%s.Cmp(%s) > 0"  pure)
    (prim big>= (big big) bool expr "%s.Cmp(%s) >= 0" pure)
    (prim big=  (big big) bool expr "%s.Cmp(%s) == 0" pure)

    ; WIDENING IS THE ONLY DIRECTION. A word value entering a big computation is
    ; converted here; the other way round is refused by the type checker, which
    ; is unbounded-rung.md §3 -- the promotion is a WIDENING, not a refinement,
    ; so it may not happen silently.
    (prim big-of (int) big expr "big.NewInt(int64(%s))" pure (import "math/big"))
    (prim big-str (big) string expr "%s.String()" pure)

    ; ---- THE DESTINATION FORMS, and Go is the only one of the four hosts that
    ; can have them.
    ;
    ; `math/big` operations WRITE INTO THEIR RECEIVER and return it, so a caller
    ; that owns its temporaries allocates nothing per step. Java's `BigInteger`
    ; is immutable and the JDK's mutable version is package-private;
    ; JavaScript's `BigInt` is a primitive. So this is not a Go optimisation we
    ; could port -- it is a Go CAPABILITY, and the other two hosts declare
    ; nothing here because there is nothing to declare.
    ;
    ; The first argument is the DESTINATION. `%s.Add(%s, %s)` is `z.Add(x, y)`,
    ; and `math/big`'s contract explicitly permits `z` to alias `x` or `y` --
    ; every operation reads its operands before writing the receiver. That
    ; guarantee is what makes `acc.Mul(acc, k)` legal, and it is the host fact
    ; the reuse rule in emit/bigreuse.go rests on.
    ;
    ; They are IMPURE, and that is load-bearing rather than pedantic: an
    ; operation that writes into an object it was handed is exactly what ADR
    ; 0010's effect discipline exists to keep in order. Declaring one pure would
    ; let the reducer substitute a destination form into two places.
    (prim big+! (big big big) big expr "%s.Add(%s, %s)")
    (prim big-! (big big big) big expr "%s.Sub(%s, %s)")
    (prim big*! (big big big) big expr "%s.Mul(%s, %s)")
    (prim big/! (big big big) big expr "%s.Quo(%s, %s)")
    (prim big%! (big big big) big expr "%s.Rem(%s, %s)")
    ; Widening into an object that already exists, which is what removes the
    ; other allocation in a loop: `acc.Mul(acc, big.NewInt(int64(i)))` still
    ; allocates once an iteration for the multiplicand.
    (prim big-of! (big int) big expr "%s.SetInt64(int64(%s))")
    ; THE FIXED-LIMB RUNG'S CARRY CHECK (emit/bignum.oro). A declared width
    ; TRUNCATES where the host's bignum stays exact, so selecting a
    ; representation would change the answer -- ADR 0009's rule at a different
    ; boundary. `trap-if` returns 0 when handed 0 and fails loudly otherwise.
    ;
    ; A func literal called immediately, exactly as the checked arithmetic in
    ; builtin.oro is written, so each operand appears once and Go's inliner sees
    ; straight-line code with a never-taken branch.
    (prim trap-if (int) int expr "func(oroC int) int { if oroC != 0 { panic(\"bignum overflow: the declared range is too small\") }; return 0 }(%s)")))
